#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139401} :activity/id #uuid "569e4d38-cbeb-5600-84fb-dbd51e15f324" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050652 :topic/id #uuid "5cc926a9-ba53-5856-be3e-b76a89d0e371" :topic/math-academy-id 2271} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139401 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139601 :tutorial/content "A **sequence** is a list of numbers.\n\nFor example, the following list of numbers is a sequence:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{4}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{8}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{12}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{16}}\n$$\n\nThe numbers in a sequence are called **terms**. For this sequence:\n\n- the **first term** is ${\\color{blue}{4}}$\n- the **second term** is ${\\color{blue}{8}}$\n- the **third term** is ${\\color{blue}{12}}$\n- the **fourth term** is ${\\color{blue}{16}}$. This is also called the **last term** of the sequence." :tutorial/id #uuid "53324f32-b9d6-59c3-a2f8-10ece4c679b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1515 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5b4ad5cd-9f94-5e15-8d5d-e677b1bbdd10" :step/math-academy-id 467030 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139402}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139402 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138064 :question/id #uuid "96401904-3d0f-56ac-ba31-eee3cbb016cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-1153"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d85ef216-095d-589d-8d1d-5a1543d576a3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138821}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Terms of a Sequence Given as a List"} :step/id #uuid "114fbb41-4b1a-5e6b-90d4-e4762b953c6b" :step/math-academy-id 467031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139403}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139741 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the following sequence:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{4}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{8}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{12}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{16}}\n$$\n\nFor this sequence:\n\n- the ${\\color{red}{1}}$st term is ${\\color{blue}{4}}$\n- the ${\\color{red}{2}}$nd term is ${\\color{blue}{8}}$\n- the ${\\color{red}{3}}$rd term is ${\\color{blue}{12}}$\n- the ${\\color{red}{4}}$th term is ${\\color{blue}{16}}$\n\nWe can represent our sequence using a mapping diagram, as shown below.\n\n![](images/55/55ce8f88af49913a493e6f9062f9f72c01f5cc707dc7f87e1862b750f696f7a0.png)\n\nThe input values refer to the position of each term in the sequence.\n\nNotice that each input value is mapped to one (and only one) output value. Therefore, this sequence is also a *function*, which we can call $f({\\color{red}{n}})$. For sequences, we usually use the letter $n$ instead of $x$ for the input variable.\n\nSo, for this function, we have the following input and output values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= 4 \\\\\nf(2) &= 8 \\\\\nf(3) &= 12 \\\\\nf(4) &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c509ec19-1e00-5896-bb05-2250f03eba71" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9415 :tutorial/title "Sequences as Functions"} :step/id #uuid "67a6436d-24b5-5814-810a-77cd3abeff9f" :step/math-academy-id 467034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139404}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139404 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139742 :tutorial/content "Let's find the sequence generated by the following function:\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{n}}) = {\\color{red}{n}} + 1\n$$\n\nWe can find the first five terms of this sequence by substituting\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{n}} = {\\color{red}{1}},{\\color{red}{2}}, {\\color{red}{3}},{\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{red}{5}}\n$$\n\ninto the function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= 1 + 1 = 2 \\\\\nf(2) &= 2 + 1 = 3 \\\\\nf(3) &= 3 + 1 = 4 \\\\\nf(4) &= 4 + 1 = 5 \\\\\nf(5) &= 5 + 1 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this function generates the following sequence:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{3}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{4}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{5}}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{6}}, \\qquad \\ldots\n$$\n\nThe \"$\\ldots$\" at the end tells us this sequence continues forever. In other words, this sequence has no last term!\n\nIf we want to find, say, the ${\\color{red}{100}}$th term of the sequence, we simply substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{n}}= {\\color{red}{100}}\n$$\n\ninto our function:\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{100}}) = {\\color{red}{100}} + 1 = {\\color{blue}{101}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ${\\color{red}{100}}$th term of the sequence is ${\\color{blue}{101}}$.\n\nWe usually need to specify which values of ${\\color{red}{n}}$ are allowed to be used in our function. To do this, we can write our function as follows:\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{n}}) = {\\color{red}{n}} + 1, \\qquad {\\color{red}{n}} \\geq 1\n$$\n\nThe statement\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{n}} \\geq 1\n$$\n\nspecifies the function's *domain*. It tells us we can input integer values greater than or equal to $1$ into our function.\n\nFinally, since every term of the sequence can be generated using our function, we say that $f({\\color{red}{n}})$ *generates the ${\\color{red}{n}}$th term of the sequence*." :tutorial/id #uuid "346e01df-270b-5fa8-bca6-2bc7dcd2db4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9416 :tutorial/title "Sequences With No Last Term"} :step/id #uuid "944a64f0-e275-5e63-945c-ce7e2fb307c9" :step/math-academy-id 467035 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139405}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138066 :question/id #uuid "5060bada-52d2-5fef-b103-e375eb568c40" :question/math-academy-id "e-1156"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c747ef7-8eec-5cbe-be45-98c4023db36c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138824}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Sequence Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "84b9847a-4d87-5ca7-a616-319ea50a12cc" :step/math-academy-id 467036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139406}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139406 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139600 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following sequence once more:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{3}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{4}},\\qquad {\\color{blue}{5}}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{6}}, \\qquad \\ldots\n$$\n\nMathematicians sometimes prefer not to use function notation when describing sequences. The alternative is to use **sequence notation**. With sequence notation, we refer to the ${\\color{red}{n}}$th term of the sequence as $a_{\\color{red}{n}}$.\n\nSo for the sequence listed above, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 2 \\\\\na_{2} &= 3 \\\\\na_{3} &= 4 \\\\\na_{4} &= 5 \\\\\na_{5} &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that we were able to express this sequence using function notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{n}}) = {\\color{red}{n}}+1, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nIn sequence notation, the formula looks similar:\n\n$$\na_{\\color{red}{n}} = {\\color{red}{n}}+1, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the ${\\color{red}{99}}$th term of our sequence by substituting\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{n}}=99\n$$\n\ninto the expression $a_{\\color{red}{n}}{:}$\n\n$$\na_{\\color{red}{99}} = {\\color{red}{99}}+1 = {\\color{blue}{100}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ${\\color{red}{99}}$th term of our sequence is ${\\color{blue}{100}}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "0aec8c6a-cd2e-56ac-8cf3-66e80c9277b5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1514 :tutorial/title "Sequence Notation"} :step/id #uuid "b30c947a-5f17-511c-8a53-9276e3ed073b" :step/math-academy-id 467040 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139407}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139407 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138457 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138065 :question/id #uuid "e9ccec31-a359-57b6-9817-9460a5b53d51" :question/math-academy-id "e-1155"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83b12b3a-2b6d-5fc4-80bb-c83415486b29" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048938}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138823}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Sequence Using Sequence Notation"} :step/id #uuid "930ebf56-2540-5a61-9df9-dc246894aa9c" :step/math-academy-id 467041}] :activity/title "Introduction to Sequences" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138598}]}
